      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good point, I\u0027ll do that. The primary reason that it wasn\u0027t done is that it wasn\u0027t strictly required. The issue with `INT64_MIN` was that C doesn\u0027t actually deal with negative literal integers. So when you specify a negative integer it first takes the numerical portion and interprets it as a positive signed value, then applies the unary `-` operator. However if we\u0027re specifying `-9223372036854775808` then it overflows as a positive signed so it attempts to promote it to an unsigned value and throws a warning. Specifying it as a `-9223372036854775807 - 1` ensures that it fits in a signed value, applies the unary `-`, then successfully subtracts 1. For the `INT32_MIN` case it wasn\u0027t needed because the compiler can promote to an `int64_t` instead.",
      "parentUuid": "435e4976_62967352",
